    {
      "api": "sec_protocol_options_are_equal()",
      "effect": "the settings the port HOLDS, compared with each other: equal when both were given the same version range, different when they were not, true for the same object, true for two NULLs, false for one. Measured: both-empty equal, same range equal, different range different, same object, null cases and the ARC balance all agree. The equality is the port's own state and NOTHING ELSE - a comparison against the release's stack would compare nothing, because there is no such stack",
      "facts": "facts/Security/SecProtocolWorklist.md",
      "introduced": "13.0",
      "kind": "function",
      "minimum": "6.0",
      "reason": "a GETTER that reads back what was put, which is the one thing the port can answer truthfully; every setter on the same object is inert, and the comparator is implemented because it reads the setters' held state rather than the release's",
      "source": "SDK 16.4, Security",
      "status": "implemented"
    },
    {
      "api": "sec_protocol_options_get_default_max_dtls_protocol_version()",
      "effect": "tls_protocol_version_DTLSv10 = 0xfeff. DTLS 1.0 measured at the upper end, and it is the SAME value as the lower one: the release's datagram context offers DTLS 1.0 and nothing above it, so the range is a single version. MEASURED on 6.1.3 and on 6.0, from the release's own SSL stack: a fresh SSLCreateContext stream context and a datagram context, with SSLGetProtocolVersionMin and SSLGetProtocolVersionMax, the two probe runs agreeing exactly. The two logs' sha256 are in facts/Security/SecProtocolDefaults.md",
      "facts": "facts/Security/SecProtocolWorklist.md",
      "introduced": "13.0",
      "kind": "function",
      "minimum": "6.0",
      "reason": "the value is the release's own stack, MEASURED on 6.1.3 and 6.0 with a probe that asks the release rather than assuming: a stream context for TLS, a datagram context for DTLS, and SSLSetProtocolVersionMax(kTLSProtocol13) for the 1.3 question",
      "source": "SDK 16.4, Security header text, plus the coordinator decision from the release stack",
      "status": "implemented"
    },
    {
      "api": "sec_protocol_options_get_default_max_tls_protocol_version()",
      "effect": "tls_protocol_version_TLSv12 = 0x0303. TLS 1.2 measured; TLS 1.3 IS REFUSED BY THE RELEASE, which was asked rather than assumed: SSLSetProtocolVersionMax(kTLSProtocol13) returns -9830 errSSLIllegalParam and the maximum then reads back as TLS 1.2, so the row is not declining to mention 1.3 - the release declines it. MEASURED on 6.1.3 and on 6.0, from the release's own SSL stack: a fresh SSLCreateContext stream context and a datagram context, with SSLGetProtocolVersionMin and SSLGetProtocolVersionMax, the two probe runs agreeing exactly. The two logs' sha256 are in facts/Security/SecProtocolDefaults.md",
      "facts": "facts/Security/SecProtocolWorklist.md",
      "introduced": "13.0",
      "kind": "function",
      "minimum": "6.0",
      "reason": "the value is the release's own stack, MEASURED on 6.1.3 and 6.0 with a probe that asks the release rather than assuming: a stream context for TLS, a datagram context for DTLS, and SSLSetProtocolVersionMax(kTLSProtocol13) for the 1.3 question",
      "source": "SDK 16.4, Security header text, plus the coordinator decision from the release stack",
      "status": "implemented"
    },
    {
      "api": "sec_protocol_options_get_default_min_dtls_protocol_version()",
      "effect": "tls_protocol_version_DTLSv10 = 0xfeff. DTLS 1.0 measured at the lower end, from a datagram context: a STREAM context answers about TLS only, and asking it about DTLS would have measured nothing. MEASURED on 6.1.3 and on 6.0, from the release's own SSL stack: a fresh SSLCreateContext stream context and a datagram context, with SSLGetProtocolVersionMin and SSLGetProtocolVersionMax, the two probe runs agreeing exactly. The two logs' sha256 are in facts/Security/SecProtocolDefaults.md",
      "facts": "facts/Security/SecProtocolWorklist.md",
      "introduced": "13.0",
      "kind": "function",
      "minimum": "6.0",
      "reason": "the value is the release's own stack, MEASURED on 6.1.3 and 6.0 with a probe that asks the release rather than assuming: a stream context for TLS, a datagram context for DTLS, and SSLSetProtocolVersionMax(kTLSProtocol13) for the 1.3 question",
      "source": "SDK 16.4, Security header text, plus the coordinator decision from the release stack",
      "status": "implemented"
    },
    {
      "api": "sec_protocol_options_get_default_min_tls_protocol_version()",
      "effect": "the enum's lowest member, tls_protocol_version_TLSv10 = 0x0301. THE RELEASE'S DEFAULT IS SSL 3.0 and tls_protocol_version_t HAS NO SSLv3 MEMBER, so the row returns the lowest member the enum can express. MEASURED on 6.1.3 and on 6.0, from the release's own SSL stack: a fresh SSLCreateContext stream context and a datagram context, with SSLGetProtocolVersionMin and SSLGetProtocolVersionMax, the two probe runs agreeing exactly. The two logs' sha256 are in facts/Security/SecProtocolDefaults.md. Recorded as a crutch: the toolchain: Security sec_protocol_options_get_default_min_tls_protocol_version cannot return the release's default",
      "facts": "facts/Security/SecProtocolWorklist.md",
      "introduced": "13.0",
      "kind": "function",
      "minimum": "6.0",
      "reason": "the value is the release's own stack, MEASURED on 6.1.3 and 6.0 with a probe that asks the release rather than assuming: a stream context for TLS, a datagram context for DTLS, and SSLSetProtocolVersionMax(kTLSProtocol13) for the 1.3 question",
      "source": "SDK 16.4, Security header text, plus the coordinator decision from the release stack",
      "status": "implemented"
    },
